WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
INTRO:
Amazon just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:01.200 --> 00:00:08.400
posted their first loss in 7 years of $3.8&nbsp;
billion. This caused Amazon stock to tumble&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:13.760
14% in a single day which is their&nbsp;
biggest one-day drop since 2006.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.320 --> 00:00:20.080
This drop itself paints quite a bleak picture, but&nbsp;
if you zoom out on Amazon stock, you’ll see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:25.920
the stock has been struggling for quite some&nbsp;
time now. It’s not that Amazon has crashed 50&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:32.720
or 70% like Netflix or PayPal. However, they’ve&nbsp;
just been going sideways for roughly 2 years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:38.240
This doesn’t sound that bad until you consider&nbsp;
that all of their peers were going parabolic till&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:44.080
the start of this year. For example, since the&nbsp;
start of 2019, Google has outperformed Amazon by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.080 --> 00:00:51.840
80%, Microsoft has outperformed Amazon by 120%&nbsp;
and Apple has outperformed Amazon by 230%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.640 --> 00:00:57.280
To make things worse, Amazon just broke down&nbsp;
from it’s sideways channel which has resulted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.280 --> 00:01:03.280
in nearly a 40% sell off from their all time&nbsp;
highs. And given the size of Amazon, this sell off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:10.960
correlates to a $745 billion loss in market cap.&nbsp;
I don’t think Mr. Bezos is a big fan of this move&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:17.040
given that he is personally down $50 billion&nbsp;
year to date. So, what happened to Amazon?

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:23.040
LACK OF GROWTH:&nbsp;

00:01:23.040 --> 00:01:28.160
Starting with the most notable concern regarding&nbsp;
Amazon, we have underwhelming growth figures. In&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:32.720
their last quarterly report, Amazon projected&nbsp;
that second quarter revenue would only grow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:39.440
3 to 7% compared to last year. Not only is this a&nbsp;
smaller growth percentage than even the S&amp;P 500,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:46.080
but more importantly it’s lower than what analysts&nbsp;
estimated. Analysts were hoping for a $125.5&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:54.080
billion Q2, but with only 3 to 7% growth, we’re&nbsp;
looking at closer to $116 to $121 billion. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.080 --> 00:02:00.400
marks the slowest growth for Amazon since the&nbsp;
dotcom crash way back in 2001. And at this rate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.400 --> 00:02:05.520
it won’t be long before Amaon ends up posting&nbsp;
a full year of single digit growth figures.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.080 --> 00:02:11.120
Aside from nearly non-existent revenue growth,&nbsp;
it seems like Amazon is also struggling with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.120 --> 00:02:16.720
prime member growth as well. This is really not&nbsp;
that surprising given that basically everyone who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.720 --> 00:02:22.880
wants prime already has it. Also, this isn’t&nbsp;
some new phenomenon either. Prime users have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:28.160
been leveling off for many years now, and it&nbsp;
looks like it’s just gonna slow down even more.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:33.200
Amazon hasn’t really made this issue much better&nbsp;
given that they’ve been constantly hiking prices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:39.120
For example, you might’ve recently heard that&nbsp;
Prime subscriptions are going up to $140 per year.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.120 --> 00:02:44.640
But, for a significant number of Prime members,&nbsp;
this price hike is even more painful. You see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:51.280
that $140 number is for annual subscription&nbsp;
holders, but the thing is, 52% of subscribers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.280 --> 00:02:57.360
pay for a monthly subscription. And the monthly&nbsp;
subscription actually costs $15 per month or $180&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:02.640
per year. Now, personally, I don’t think we can&nbsp;
really blame Amazon for this fee increase given&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.640 --> 00:03:08.000
the rampant inflation we’ve been experiencing, but&nbsp;
that doesn’t really matter. Whether Amazon’s price&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:13.200
hikes are justified or not, their membership cost&nbsp;
is simply becoming unaffordable or not worth it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.200 --> 00:03:19.280
for many people. And this is extremely bad news&nbsp;
for Amazon given that their a growth company.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:22.880
I think a lot of people forgot about this&nbsp;
given all of the hype surrounding Tesla&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.880 --> 00:03:28.640
stock and it’s sky high PE ratio. But, before&nbsp;
Tesla stock went to the moon, Amazon was by far&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:34.720
the biggest growth company in the world. After&nbsp;
all, Amazon has regularly had a PE ratio of 100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.720 --> 00:03:39.520
or higher for most of their history. It wasn’t&nbsp;
till the last few years that Amazon’s PE ratio&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:46.160
has consistently come in at less than 100. But,&nbsp;
even now, Amazon’s PE ratio stands in the mid 50s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:51.840
which is extremely high for a company as mature&nbsp;
as Amazon. And given their poor growth forecasts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.840 --> 00:03:57.680
it’s very possible that Amazon is even more mature&nbsp;
than we originally thought. Amazon is literally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.680 --> 00:04:03.760
posting growth numbers that are worse than Apple,&nbsp;
Google, and Microsoft, yet their PE ratio is more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:09.040
than 2 times higher than any of these companies.&nbsp;
By this logic, Amazon stock could drop another 50%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:14.320
and it would still be more richly valued than&nbsp;
the other FAANG companies. And unless, Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:19.120
is able to get their growth story back on track,&nbsp;
it’s likely that Amazon’s pain has just begun.

00:04:19.120 --> 00:04:23.360
RISING COSTS:&nbsp;

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:28.400
Usually, companies like to substitute for a lack&nbsp;
of revenue growth with strong growth in profits,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:34.320
and that’s what Amazon was trying to do as well.&nbsp;
Till the end 2017, Amazon’s net income was a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.320 --> 00:04:39.040
laughable amount compared to their revenue given&nbsp;
that their net margin was literally less than 1%.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:44.960
This is because, Jeff Bezos preferred to reinvest&nbsp;
all of their excess income back into the business,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.960 --> 00:04:50.800
but in late 2017, he had a change of heart. He&nbsp;
finally decided to turn on the profit switch and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.800 --> 00:04:56.320
Amazon’s net income went through the roof until&nbsp;
now. Now, the most astute of you point out that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.320 --> 00:05:01.600
Amazon wouldn’t have even lost money this quarter&nbsp;
if it wasn’t for their $7.6 billion write off on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:07.680
Rivian. And while this is absolutely true, even&nbsp;
if we ignore this, their bottom line isn’t looking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.680 --> 00:05:12.240
that great. If we take a look at their quarterly&nbsp;
report, we’ll see that total net sales jumped from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:19.920
$108 billion to $116 billion from Q1 of 2021 to&nbsp;
Q1 of 2022. Yet despite this, their operating&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:26.400
income over halved from $8.8 billion to $3.6&nbsp;
billion. And it’s not like this reduction can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:31.600
be attributed to any one factor either. Rather,&nbsp;
we’re seeing Amazon’s operating costs increase&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:37.040
across the board. Also, it’s not like these&nbsp;
increases are that substantial either. We’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:43.200
looking 10% in one category and 15% in another,&nbsp;
but given Amazon’s single digit net margin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:49.840
these increases add up quickly. And the worst part&nbsp;
is that most of this is not under their control.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.840 --> 00:05:56.480
For example, their cost of sales increased from&nbsp;
$62.4 billion to $66.5 billion. If you’re not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:01.200
familiar with cost of sales, it’s basically just&nbsp;
the cost to produce a given product or service.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.200 --> 00:06:05.520
And given that the prices of raw materials and&nbsp;
semi conductors are increasing substantially,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:12.480
this is not that suprising. Similarly, Amazon’s&nbsp;
fulfillment costs rose from $16.5 billion to $20.2&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:18.400
billion. Likely one of the biggest factors driving&nbsp;
up this category is skyrocketing oil prices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:23.520
Moving onto their next category, we see a very&nbsp;
similar story. Technology and content rose from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:30.160
$12.48 billion to $14.8 billion. This can also&nbsp;
likely be attributed to rising chip costs which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:35.040
have resulted in more expensive databases and&nbsp;
servers. In the end, Amazon is basically getting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:40.720
screwed by inflation like all of us, but just on a&nbsp;
much bigger scale. As a business, their long term&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.720 --> 00:06:45.600
goal would be to pass on these costs to customers,&nbsp;
and that’s likely what they’re trying to do, but&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:50.800
it’s a pretty fragile balance between maintaining&nbsp;
customers while increasing costs. Fortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.800 --> 00:06:54.880
Jerome Powell continues to insist that we’re&nbsp;
gonna have a soft landing and that the worst of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.880 --> 00:07:00.080
inflation is behind us. The market doesn’t quite&nbsp;
believe him, but Jerome being right would not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.080 --> 00:07:06.720
only be great for Amazon but for all of us. So,&nbsp;
hopefully, he’s right, but even if he is right,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.720 --> 00:07:11.040
it would take a few quarters for stabilizing&nbsp;
inflation to reflect in Amazon’s bottom line.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:15.840
So, Amazon will likely have to deal with&nbsp;
rising costs till atleast the end of this year.

00:07:15.840 --> 00:07:19.440
PHYSICAL RETAILERS:&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:24.880
Aside from all these internal problems Amazon has&nbsp;
been facing, they’re also facing a major external&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.880 --> 00:07:30.080
problem which is physical retailers. For decades,&nbsp;
physical retailers weren’t much of a threat for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:35.360
Amazon. In fact, it was usually the other way&nbsp;
around. Physical retailers were scared of losing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:40.480
market share to Amazon as they navigated the&nbsp;
retail apocalypse. And this was just made worse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.480 --> 00:07:45.840
by the pandemic and stay at home orders. However,&nbsp;
as things return to normal, it seems like the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.840 --> 00:07:51.520
innovative physical retailers are rising stronger&nbsp;
than ever before. In fact, online retailers lost&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:56.080
ground to physical retailers in 2021 and I&nbsp;
have a full video about this phenomenon if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.080 --> 00:08:00.080
you’re interested. But, the main explanation&nbsp;
for this shift is that physical retailers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.080 --> 00:08:04.720
have learned to offer the best of both worlds.&nbsp;
Historically, physical retailers were trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.720 --> 00:08:09.040
compete head on with Amazon by simply creating&nbsp;
their own websites and home delivery services&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.040 --> 00:08:14.160
which were usually subpar to Amazon’s. However,&nbsp;
the innovative retailers have since learned to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.160 --> 00:08:19.280
leverage their locations to offer something that&nbsp;
Amazon can’t. This includes convenient returns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.280 --> 00:08:23.680
curb side pickup, no delivery fees, knowledgeable&nbsp;
customer service, and a much more interactive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.680 --> 00:08:28.640
shopping experience. Aside from all of these&nbsp;
factors, many customers have also become quite&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:33.680
weary of Amazon’s quality. The truth is that the&nbsp;
vast majority of the products on Amazon are just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.680 --> 00:08:39.760
highly marked up items from AliExpress/Alibaba.&nbsp;
And usually the quality is just not that great&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.760 --> 00:08:43.920
especially compared to items that you can&nbsp;
find at physical retailers. Personally,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:49.120
I’ve found this issue to be most prevalent amongst&nbsp;
cheap electronics and specifically dongles. Since&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:54.560
the start of 2021, I’ve gone through 3 USB C&nbsp;
dongles and 3 Display Port to HDMI convertors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.560 --> 00:09:00.800
all from Amazon. They simply stop working within&nbsp;
3 to 6 months. Considering all this, it no longer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:05.600
seems like online retailers are just going to&nbsp;
completely wipe out physical retailers. Rather,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:09.440
it seems like they’ve reached some sort of balance&nbsp;
and that things are going to be relatively stable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.440 --> 00:09:15.200
moving forward. And while this is great news for&nbsp;
physical retailers, this is terrible for Amazon.

00:09:15.200 --> 00:09:19.440
AMAZON’S DARK HORSE:&nbsp;

00:09:19.440 --> 00:09:24.720
Up until now, we’ve mainly discussed Amazon’s&nbsp;
retail business, but the truth is, a significant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:30.000
portion of their business has nothing to do&nbsp;
with retail. While 84% of Amazon’s revenue does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:34.880
come from their retail business, virtually all of&nbsp;
their profits come from their cloud business, AWS.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.520 --> 00:09:41.760
In fact, last quarter AWS accounted for 100% of&nbsp;
Amazon’s operating income given that Amazon.com&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.760 --> 00:09:46.240
was a net loss both in North America and&nbsp;
internationally. This is really nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:52.880
new given AWS has been holding up Amazon since it&nbsp;
was created in 2006. But, over the past few years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.880 --> 00:09:57.520
it did seem like Amazon was finally starting&nbsp;
to become independent of AWS as they posted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.520 --> 00:10:03.200
their first profitable quarters. However, all of&nbsp;
that progress was wiped out with rising costs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:09.680
And this leaves us with a serious question, how&nbsp;
long can AWS just keep propping up Amazon? Using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:09.680 --> 00:10:15.360
AWS to fund Amazon’s growth was a genius strategy&nbsp;
in the early 2010s. But, it’s starting to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:21.680
old now that we’re 16 years in. Fortunately, it&nbsp;
doesn’t seem like AWS revenue is slowing down yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:25.440
but it’s only a matter of time until&nbsp;
any business starts to stagnate.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:31.840
And Amazon’s continuing dependence on AWS&nbsp;
isn’t the most assuring of characteristics.

00:10:31.840 --> 00:10:34.160
FUTURE OUTLOOK:&nbsp;

00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:39.760
Despite all these concerns, I’m personally still&nbsp;
optimistic about Amazon’s future. Here’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:47.520
thing, Amazon has decisively won ecomerce and&nbsp;
yes they’re a monopoly. 56.7% of all e-commerce&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.520 --> 00:10:53.200
sales happened on Amazon in 2021, and while&nbsp;
online retailing is starting to stabilize,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.200 --> 00:10:57.680
I don’t think Amazon will be losing their&nbsp;
dominance within this sector anytime soon.&nbsp;

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:02.160
Something else to note is that Amazon just&nbsp;
announced a 20 to 1 stock split and this would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:08.000
be their first stock split since 1999. At current&nbsp;
prices, Amazon’s post split price would be just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:13.200
over $100. And though, stock splits don’t actually&nbsp;
change anything fundamentally about the company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:18.000
I think a stock split could attract a lot more&nbsp;
retail investors. I know some of you will argue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:21.840
that the introduction of fractional shares&nbsp;
has made stock splits completely useless.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.840 --> 00:11:28.000
And technically this is true if we just focus on&nbsp;
stock returns, but I mean who actually wants to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.000 --> 00:11:33.840
own a quarter or a third of a share. Also, trading&nbsp;
derivatives on Amazon is currently unreasonably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:33.840 --> 00:11:39.280
expensive for most retail investors. A stock split&nbsp;
will substantially help with derivatives pricing.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:43.680
Combine this new retail interest with a&nbsp;
fundamental recovery from Amazon and I think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:48.560
it’s just a matter of time until Amazon recovers&nbsp;
to its all time high and makes new all time highs.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:56.160
I liked Amazon stock when it was $2900 per share,&nbsp;
so I really like it now, but that’s just what I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.160 --> 00:12:01.200
think and it’s definitely not financial advice.&nbsp;
Would you guys invest in Amazon right now? Comment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:01.200 --> 00:12:06.320
that down below. Also, drop a like if you’re&nbsp;
hoping that this stock market pain ends soon.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.320 --> 00:12:09.520
And of course, consider checking out our&nbsp;
international channels to watch our videos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:13.360
in other languages and consider subscribing&nbsp;
to see more questions logically answered.

